dict(n="Trebuchet and siege engines",y=500,when="Traction trebuchet in China (4th century BCE); counterweight trebuchet in the 12th century",where="China, Middle East, Europe",lane="Global",by="Chinese, Byzantine and Islamic engineers",group=F,wiki="Trebuchet",
 mat="Timber, rope, iron, counterweight boxes",crew="Dozens of operators",size="Could hurl 100 kg stones up to about 300 m",
 summary="Siege engines were designed to break walls.",
 steps=["Battering rams under roofed shelters (tortoises).","Siege towers with drawbridges.","Mining: digging under walls, propping tunnels with timber, then burning the props.","Counterweight trebuchets.","Gunpowder bombards from the 15th century."],
 tricks="At Stirling Castle (1304) Edward I built the trebuchet 'Warwolf' and refused the garrison's surrender until he had tested it. Besiegers also catapulted dead animals and plague victims — Mongols at Caffa (1346) reportedly did so.",legacy="Ended by cannon.",conn="Mongol conquests; Black Death; How castle defences worked"),
